 HISTORY                                                          3006 1451
 -------                                                          3006 1451
 This evaluation for delta-zirconium hydride was generated at the 3006 1451
 Naval Nuclear Laboratory (NNL) in 2023 using the FLASSH code     3006 1451
 [1]. Previous evaluations for ZrH have used a single material    3006 1451
 evaluation based on the GA phonon spectrum for ZrH2 [2] to treat 3006 1451
 both the delata and epsilon zirconium hydride phases. Separate   3006 1451
 material evaluations have been generated for delta-zirconium     3006 1451
 hydride (ZrHx) and epsilon-zirconium hydride (ZrH2) and include  3006 1451
 coherent elastic scattering for the zirconium crystal            3006 1451
 sublattices, which has been previously neglected in lieu of an   3006 1451
 incoherent elastic treatment. The FLASSH calculations, described 3006 1451
 in Ref. 3, used an ab initio phonon model and crystal structure  3006 1451
 developed within the MedeA materials simulation software [4].    3006 1451
 The valid H composition range for the ZrHx material evalutaion is3006 1451
 defined by the phase diagram, where the recommended is Ref. 5.   3006 1451
 The material evaluation of ZrHx was verfied with integral cross  3006 1451
 section measurements [3].                                        3006 1451
                                                                  3006 1451
 THEORY                                                           3006 1451
 ------                                                           3006 1451
 ZrHx has a fluorite prototype crystal structure with the         3006 1451
 zirconium atoms forming a face-centered sublattice and hydrogen  3006 1451
 atoms situated in the tetrahedral sites, which are randomly      3006 1451
 occupied with atoms or vacancies according to the stoichiometry. 3006 1451
 The phonon spectrum of H and Zr were calculated with ab initio   3006 1451
 molecular dynamics [6,7] and ab initio lattice dynamics [8,9]    3006 1451
 methods, respectively, using forcefields dervied from the VASP   3006 1451
 density functional theory code [10,11]. The PHONON code [12] was 3006 1451
 used for lattice dynamics techniques, whereas phonons were       3006 1451
 computed as the Fourier transform of the velocity autocorrelation3006 1451
 function for molecular dynamics. Details of the ab inito         3006 1451
 calculations are found in Ref. 3.                                3006 1451
                                                                  3006 1451
 H-1(ZrHx) and elemental Zr(ZrHx) evaluations were generated for  3006 1451
 77 - 1200 K. The coherent scattering lengths used to compute     3006 1451
 coherent elastic contributions to MT=2 of Zr(ZrHx) has been      3006 1451
 modified from Ref. 3 to the random alloy theory described in     3006 1451
 Ref. 13. Mixed elastic scattering treatment, LTHR=3, is used for 3006 1451
 Zr(ZrHx) with the incoherent scattering cross section in Ref. 13.3006 1451
 MT=2 of H-1(ZrHx) is evaluated as incoherent elastic only.       3006 1451
 Elemental average AWR and scattering cross sections were computed3006 1451
 from ENDF/B-VIII.1 nuclide evaluations (see Ref. 3) with isotopic3006 1451
 abundances from NIST [14]. Total scattering cross sections       3006 1451
 were computed from MF=3, MT=2 at 0.0253 eV and supplemented with 3006 1451
 NIST [15]. Inelastic scattering on MT=4 was generated in the     3006 1451
 incoherent approximation with a phonon order of 300.             3006 1451
                                                                  3006 1451
 The calculated lattice parameter of a=0.477 nm agrees well with  3006 1451
 x-ray diffraction measurements at room temperature [16]. The     3006 1451
 H(ZrHx) phonon spectrum is in reasonable agreement with inelastic3006 1451
 neutron scattering measurements and has consistent trends        3006 1451
 relative to H(ZrHx) [17]. The integrated cross section for the   3006 1451
 ZrHx TSL agrees with neutron transmission measurements [18,19].  3006 1451
                                                                  3006 1451
